\section{Main Results: Generalization (\texorpdfstring{Vancouver $\rightarrow$ Cologne}{Vancouver to Cologne})}
\label{sec:results_main}
Table~\ref{tab:gen_stats} reports the generalization performance of the Vancouver-trained region-aware MARL\_DQN controller on the Cologne map over 120 independent episodes. Following the reporting style in the reference literature, we present mean \(\pm\) standard deviation and the 95\% confidence interval.

\begin{table}[h]
    \centering
    \caption{Generalization results on Cologne over \(n=120\) seeded episodes (mean \(\pm\) std, with 95\% CI).}
    \label{tab:gen_stats}
    \begin{tabular}{l c c}
        \hline
        \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Mean \(\pm\) Std} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
        \hline
        Avg. waiting time (s) & \(103.78 \pm 1.03\) & \([103.60,\;103.97]\) \\
        Avg. queue length (veh) & \(3008.97 \pm 10.94\) & \([3007.01,\;3010.92]\) \\
        Throughput (veh/h) & \(511.98 \pm 33.24\) & \([506.03,\;517.92]\) \\
        Congestion index (-) & \(0.7568 \pm 0.0089\) & \([0.7552,\;0.7584]\) \\
        \hline
        Total CO\(_2\) (mg) & \(7.84\times 10^{9} \pm 2.76\times 10^{7}\) & \([7.8346,\;7.8445]\times 10^{9}\) \\
        Total fuel (ml) & \(2.53\times 10^{9} \pm 8.90\times 10^{6}\) & \([2.53195,\;2.53514]\times 10^{9}\) \\
        \hline
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Interpretation.} The controller achieves an average waiting time of \(103.78\) seconds with low variance across seeds, indicating stable performance under stochastic demand realizations. The throughput variability (\(\pm 33\) veh/h) suggests sensitivity to episode-level randomness (e.g., departures, teleports, and routing), which is expected in large OSM networks. Importantly, emissions totals remain within a narrow band (approximately \(\approx 1.8\%\) range across episodes), supporting the claim that the evaluation is consistent and not dominated by rare outlier runs.

These preliminary results demonstrate that the proposed MARL\_DQN controller successfully generalizes from the Vancouver training environment to the unseen Cologne city map, achieving consistent performance across 120 independent episodes. The low variance in key metrics (waiting time, queue length, emissions) indicates that the learned policy is stable and robust to stochastic traffic demand variations.

\section{Statistical Tests and Baseline Comparisons}
\label{sec:results_tests}
To provide a comprehensive evaluation of the proposed MARL\_DQN controller, a full comparative analysis against baseline controllers is planned. The evaluation protocol will include: (i) baseline performance means computed under the same seed configuration as the MARL\_DQN runs, (ii) pairwise statistical tests (independent t-test and Mann--Whitney U test) to assess significance of performance differences, and (iii) effect size analysis (e.g., absolute seconds saved per vehicle, percentage improvement in throughput) to quantify practical relevance.

The baseline controllers to be evaluated include Fixed-Time Controller (FTC), Adaptive Controller, MaxPressure Controller, SOTL (Self-Organizing Traffic Lights), and Genetic Algorithm (GA). All baseline runs will use the same evaluation setup as described in Table~\ref{tab:results_setup}, ensuring fair comparison under identical traffic demand, simulation horizon, and controlled intersection selection.

\section{Remaining Work and Future Improvement}
\label{sec:results_future}
Current work focuses on finalizing the evaluation of comparison baselines (FTC, Adaptive, MaxPressure, SOTL, GA) to enable the statistical testing described in Section~\ref{sec:results_tests}. These baseline controllers have been implemented and integrated into the simulation framework, but their full evaluation across 120 independent episodes on the Cologne map is still in progress. Once completed, the comparative results will enable rigorous statistical analysis to determine whether the MARL\_DQN controller achieves significant improvements over rule-based and optimization-based baselines.

In addition to baseline comparisons, several components of the system have been identified for further improvement and will be addressed in the Final Report:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Comprehensive baseline evaluation}: Complete the 120-episode evaluation runs for all baseline controllers (FTC, Adaptive, MaxPressure, SOTL, GA) on the Cologne map to enable full statistical comparison.
    
    \item \textbf{Ablation studies}: Conduct ablation studies to isolate the contribution of key design choices, including:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Region-aware reward shaping: compare \(\lambda=0\) vs \(\lambda=0.01\) to quantify the impact of regional cooperation.
        \item Lane padding \(K\): evaluate \(K\in\{4,8,12\}\) to study the trade-off between state compression and representation fidelity.
        \item Controlled traffic lights ratio: vary controlled ratio (e.g., 0.1, 0.2, 0.3) to assess scalability to larger networks.
        \item Switching penalty coefficient: vary the switch penalty to quantify the balance between stability and responsiveness.
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item \textbf{Multi-city evaluation}: Extend evaluation to additional OSM city maps (e.g., Los Angeles) to further assess generalization capability across diverse urban topologies.
    
    \item \textbf{Training analysis}: Provide detailed training curves, convergence analysis, and hyperparameter sensitivity studies to document the learning dynamics of the MARL\_DQN agent.
\end{itemize}

The full comparative results, statistical analysis, and ablation studies will be presented in the Final Report, providing a complete assessment of the proposed traffic signal control system's performance relative to established baselines.

